<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In early 694 the caliph Abd al-Malik made al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf, who had besieged Ibn al-Zubayr in Mecca and then governed the Hejaz, governor of Iraq, the most important post of the Islamic state. Kufa was in disorder: on the death of the last governor the troops of Kufa and Basra serving under al-Muhallab had left their camp and were idling in the cities. Al-Hajjaj gave his inaugural sermon in the mosque.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>I see before me heads ripe for the sickle, and the reaper—I am he. It seems to me, as if I saw already the blood between your turbans and your shoulders.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">The opening of the sermon, as translated by Michael Jan de Goeje in the Encyclopaedia Britannica, 11th edition (1911), article Caliphate.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“I see before me heads ripe for the sickle, and the reaper—I am he. It seems to me, as if I saw already the blood between your turbans and your shoulders.” — al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf, Al-Hajjaj's Sermon at Kufa" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-hajjaj-at-kufa.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>He ordered every man who had not returned to the camp within three days to be put to death and his property plundered, and the troops went back. Sent against the Kharijites, they defeated the Azariqa in 696 and another Kharijite leader in 697, but his pay cuts and the unpopular campaigns helped drive the Iraqis into the revolt of Ibn al-Ash'ath in 699, which nearly destroyed Umayyad power in Iraq.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azariqa">Azariqa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-ashath">Ibn al-Ash'ath</a></div></div></section>
